You can use a loop to achieve this in C++. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 20, c = 30;

    // Using a loop to run the same code on multiple variables
    for (auto& var : {a, b, c}) {
        var *= 2;
        std::cout << var << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the code inside the loop will be executed for each variable in the initializer list `{a, b, c}`. The `auto& var` syntax is used to get a reference to each variable in the list, allowing us to modify it inside the loop.

Alternatively, you can use a range-based for loop with a lambda function to achieve the same result:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 20, c = 30;

    // Using a lambda function to run the same code on multiple variables
    [](auto& var) { var *= 2; }(a);
    [](auto& var) { var *= 2; }(b);
    [](auto& var) { var *= 2; }(c);

    std::cout << a << std::endl;
    std::cout << b << std::endl;
    std::cout << c << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

However, this approach is less concise and less readable than the first example.

You can also use a function to run the same code on multiple variables:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

void multiplyByTwo(int& var) {
    var *= 2;
}

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 20, c = 30;

    // Using a function to run the same code on multiple variables
    multiplyByTwo(a);
    multiplyByTwo(b);
    multiplyByTwo(c);

    std::cout << a << std::endl;
    std::cout << b << std::endl;
    std::cout << c << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This approach is more readable and maintainable than the previous examples, but it requires more code.

